// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The catalog as a reference table.
//
// A Log Analytics custom table is append-only. There is no UPDATE and no DELETE,
// so "the catalog" is not the set of rows in AppCatalog_CL -- it is the newest
// row per workload, minus anything that has been tombstoned.
//
// Two bookkeeping columns make that expressible (both written by sync-catalog.mjs):
//
//   isActive   false marks a tombstone. Without it, removing a service from the
//              source would be invisible: arg_max() would keep returning its last
//              known row forever and the retired service could never leave the
//              portfolio.
//   rowHash    content hash of the business columns. The sync only writes rows
//              whose hash changed, so this table is a genuine change log rather
//              than N identical rows per day.
//
// The three queries below are the canonical shapes. The first is the one that
// matters -- everything downstream should join against it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// 1. CURRENT STATE — the catalog as it stands right now.
//    This is the query the portfolio joins against.
AppCatalog_CL
| summarize arg_max(TimeGenerated, *) by workload
| where isActive
| project workload, displayName, domain, criticality, owner, businessOwner,
          description, appUrl, docsUrl, runbookUrl, repoUrl, slo, onCall,
          LastChanged = TimeGenerated
| order by domain asc, criticality asc, workload asc


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. POINT IN TIME — what did the catalog look like during last week's incident?
//    This is the whole reason to keep the table alongside the saved function:
//    'AppCatalog' only ever knows the present.
//
// let asOf = datetime(2026-09-01 14:00:00);
// AppCatalog_CL
// | where TimeGenerated <= asOf
// | summarize arg_max(TimeGenerated, *) by workload
// | where isActive
// | project workload, displayName, owner, onCall, criticality


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. CHANGE HISTORY — every ownership change, retirement, and reactivation.
//    Because unchanged rows are never rewritten, each row here is a real event.
//
//    Keyed off rowHash rather than a single field, so any edit is caught. The
//    isnotempty(rowHash) filter skips rows written before the bookkeeping columns
//    existed; in a clean deployment it is a no-op.
//
// AppCatalog_CL
// | where isnotempty(rowHash)
// | order by workload asc, TimeGenerated asc
// | extend PrevWorkload = prev(workload), PrevHash = prev(rowHash),
//          PrevActive = prev(isActive), PrevOwner = prev(owner)
// | where workload == PrevWorkload
// | where rowHash != PrevHash or isActive != PrevActive
// | project TimeGenerated, workload,
//           Change = case(isActive == false,   strcat("retired (was ", PrevOwner, ")"),
//                         PrevActive == false, "reactivated",
//                         owner != PrevOwner,  strcat("owner: ", PrevOwner, " -> ", owner),
//                                              "details updated"),
//           syncId
// | order by TimeGenerated desc


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Note: the saved function 'AppCatalog' answers query 1 directly and needs no
// arg_max, because it is ARM metadata that gets replaced wholesale on each sync.
// It also has no retention, so it survives indefinitely. Prefer it for joins;
// use AppCatalog_CL when you need history.
//
//   AppCatalog | where isActive
